sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch13-18> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The characteristic differences between domestic breeds produced by man's selection (in dogs, horses and pigeons) generally do not appear early in life and are inherited at a corresponding later age, so that the young of different breeds differ far less in proportions than the adults, with occasional exceptions such as the short-faced tumbler pigeon.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Young of domestic breeds differ much less than adults";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XIII, “Mutual Affinities of Organic Beings: Morphology: Embryology: Rudimentary Organs”; section “EMBRYOLOGY, laws of, explained by variations not supervening at an early age, and being inherited at a corresponding age”. Scope: domestic breeds of dogs, horses and pigeons"
}
